<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The smoothed
potential <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{-V_{\eta}}\coloneqq e^{-V}*\mathsf{N}(0,\eta I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>𝖭</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-V_{\eta}}\coloneqq e^{-V}*\mathsf{N}(0,\eta I)</annotation></semantics></math>, is given by</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V_{\eta}(y)=\mathbb{E}\bigl[\nabla V(X)\bigm|X+\sqrt{\eta}\,G=y\bigr]\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V_{\eta}(y)=\mathbb{E}\bigl[\nabla V(X)\bigm|X+\sqrt{\eta}\,G=y\bigr]\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\sim\mathsf{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>𝖭</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\sim\mathsf{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\pi</annotation></semantics></math> are independent.
The smoothed potential admits quantitative higher-order derivative bounds, allowing us to apply higher-order integrators.
The catch is that (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 3.1 ‣ 3.1 The smoothing trick ‣ 3 Technical overview ‣ Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) is given as a conditional expectation, and is therefore not explicitly computable.
Write <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\eta}\coloneqq\nabla V_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\eta}\coloneqq\nabla V_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>